'''''Serissa japonica''''' is a species of [[flowering plant]]s in the family [[Rubiaceae]].<ref name = powo2/> It is the sole species in [[genus]] '''''Serissa'''''.<ref name = powo1/> It is native to open [[sub-tropical]] woodlands and [[wet meadow]]s in southeast [[Asia]], from [[India]], and [[China]] to [[Japan]]. It is commonly called the '''snowrose''', '''tree of a thousand stars''', or '''Japanese boxthorn'''. Snowrose and tree of a thousand stars are different cultivars. The only method of differentiating is measuring the difference in the shape and size of the flowers produced.{{citation needed|date=October 2016}}

==Description==
[[File:Timelapse of a Serissa japonica flower opening.webm|thumb|left|Timelapse of a ''Buchozia japonica'' flower opening.]]
''Serissa japonica'' is an [[evergreen]] or semi-evergreen [[shrub]], 45–60&nbsp;cm high, with oval, deep green, rather thick [[leaf|leaves]] that have an unpleasant smell if bruised (hence one of its synonyms, ''foetida''). The upright stems branch in all directions and form a wide bushy dome. It is grown for its neat habit, good coverage of branches and long flowering time. It is also valued for its rough, grey trunk which tends to get lighter in colour with age.

It flowers practically all year round, but particularly from early spring to near autumn. The 4- to 6-lobed [[flower]]s are funnel-shaped and 1&nbsp;cm wide. They first appear as pink [[bud]]s but turn to a profusion of white flowers. Fertilizing is especially important during the long flowering period.{{cn|date=March 2023}}

==Cultivars==
Many [[cultivar]]s with double flowers or variegated leaves are also available. 'Pink Snow Rose' has pale pink flowers and leaves edged off-white. Other cultivars include: 'Variegata', 'Variegated Pink', 'Pink Mystic', 'Snowflake', 'Snowleaves', 'Mt. Fuji', 'Kyoto' and 'Sapporo'.

==Bonsai==
[[File:Serissa.jpg|thumb|left|Bonsai version of ''Serissa japonica'']]
''Serissa japonica'' is one of the most common [[bonsai]] sold in America and Europe. It is not difficult to maintain as bonsai, but is very fussy. Many beginner bonsai enthusiasts will destroy a plant in their uninformed attempts to care for it. The trees respond adversely by dropping leaves if over-watered, under-watered, if it's too cold, too hot, or even if just moved to a new location. The plant usually grows back to health when put back to better conditions.<ref name="Ma-Ke_Serissa foetida">{{cite web|url=http://guide.makebonsai.com/bonsai_species_guide_training.asp?SpeciesID=5012&Name=Serissa_foetida|title=Ma-Ke Bonsai Care Guide for Serissa foetida|last=D'Cruz|first=Mark|date=|website=|publisher=Ma-Ke Bonsai|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121214065951/http://guide.makebonsai.com/bonsai_species_guide_training.asp?SpeciesID=5012&Name=Serissa_foetida|archive-date=2012-12-14|accessdate=2011-05-09|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="Ma-Ke_Serissa foetida Var. Mount Fuji">{{cite web|url=http://guide.makebonsai.com/bonsai_species_guide_training.asp?SpeciesID=5093&name=Serissa_foetida_Mount_Fuji|title=Ma-Ke Bonsai Care Guide for Serissa foetida Var. Mount Fuji|last=D'Cruz|first=Mark|date=|website=|publisher=Ma-Ke Bonsai|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141012194813/http://guide.makebonsai.com/bonsai_species_guide_training.asp?SpeciesID=5093&name=Serissa_foetida_Mount_Fuji|archive-date=2014-10-12|accessdate=2011-05-09|url-status=dead}}</ref>

